export const SKILL_DEFAULTS: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  "SKILL.md": "---\nname: cutting-montage-videos\ndescription: Use for every Nekter editing job - turn the clips in footage/ into short vertical clean-cut videos (reels and stories) with opening-text options and a post caption, or revise earlier cuts, and write out/manifest.json.\n---\n\n# Cutting vertical videos (cloud)\n\nYou are editing one job, unattended, in this folder. Nobody can answer while you work. Make the cuts, check\nthem, write `out/manifest.json`, and finish. Do not plan at length.\n\nFixed rules: vertical 1080x1920, 30 fps, clean cuts only (no transitions, no effects, no text burned in).\nEvery cut has music from `music/`, cut on the beat, unless the request or instructions say no music\n(or `job.json` lists no tracks): then it has no music. No voice-over is added, but a shot can keep its clip's\nown sound: when someone talks on camera, their words are part of the video (see \"Sound from the clips\").\n\n## The folder\n\n```\njob.json         the brief: kind, videoKind, brand {name, voice}, instructions, request, history, clips, music, epidemicSound\nprevious.json    revisions only: the last manifest; each EDL entry has its footage `file`, each cut its `music`\nfootage/         the clips (flat; filenames as listed in job.json)\nmusic/           the music library (filenames as listed in job.json `music`, with title, bpm, loudestFrom)\nwork/            yours: scan, listen, moments, samples, EDL drafts\nout/             manifest.json and the preview .mp4 files\n```\n\n`SK=.claude/skills/cutting-montage-videos/scripts`. Use relative paths only; never leave this folder.\nClips are only ever read; never modify or delete anything in `footage/`.\n\n## Workflow (first cut)\n\n1. **Brief**: read `job.json`. `request` is what the person asked for; `history` is what they said earlier in the\n   same conversation, oldest first, and still stands unless a later message changes it; `instructions` are the\n   workspace's own standing rules (follow them unless the request says otherwise); `brand.voice` is how they talk.\n2. **Scan, listen, find the action**: three commands, then read what they print.\n   - `python3 $SK/scan.py footage work/scan`: read every `work/scan/sheet-N.jpg` to understand the shoot. Clips\n     flagged `unreadable` are never used.\n   - `python3 $SK/listen.py footage work/listen`: which clips have someone talking, what they say and when\n     (`work/listen/speech.tsv`). A clip marked `TALKING` was filmed for its words.\n   - `python3 $SK/moments.py footage work/moments`: for every clip of 8s or more, the windows where the most\n     happens (movement and sound hits) and the quiet stretches to avoid.\n3. **Intake**: decide everything from the brief with `intake.md`. Do not ask unless it says to.\n4. **Select**: shortlist clips per cut, then `printf '%s\\n' clipA.mov clipB.mov | bash $SK/sample.sh footage work/samples/<cut>`\n   (a frame about every 5 seconds through each clip; it prints each frame's time). Read the sheets and choose\n   moments with the profile `intake.md` picks (`profiles/build.md`, `profiles/event.md` or `profiles/training.md`).\n   **Never pick a moment from one frame.** For every shot you want from a clip over 15s, start from the strongest\n   windows `moments.py` printed, then look closely at each candidate: `printf '%s\\n' 'clip.mov|41|47' | bash $SK/sample.sh footage work/samples/zoom-1`\n   (several lines at once is fine). Use it only if the frames show the moment actually happening (the hit landing,\n   the rep, the reaction), and start the shot about half a second before it. A window where people only walk,\n   reset, wait or stand is not a shot, however sharp it looks. Never take a clip from its start by default.\n   Decide per shot: a real-speed snippet (the default) or sped up (only pans and long takes, per the profile).\n5. **Assemble**: write a draft EDL per cut in `work/edl/<id>.draft.tsv`, one shot per line:\n   `clip_filename|in_seconds|source_seconds|speed|crop_x(optional)|sound(optional)` (filename as in `footage/`,\n   no folder; `sound` is `1` on a shot that keeps its clip's own sound, so `talk.mov|0.8|5.1|1||1`).\n   Then `python3 $SK/fit.py footage work/edl/<id>.draft.tsv <seconds> work/edl/<id>.tsv <pace>` (exact length,\n   bounds, no reused range, pacing). `<pace>` is `quick` (fast montage, the default) or `held` (shots run 2 to 5\n   seconds): use `held` whenever the request, `history`, `style.md` or the profile asks for longer shots, fewer cuts\n   or less choppy editing, and keep using it on every later revision of that conversation. Fix every problem it\n   reports and run it again; a `quick` pacing problem means more, shorter real-speed shots, not faster ones.\n   **Music** (unless no music is wanted): pick one track per cut from `job.json` `music` that fits the brief and\n   pace (follow any sound or music instruction in `request` or `instructions`; title and tempo are all you know;\n   prefer `beatStrength` above ~2; a track with null `bpm` can be read with `python3 $SK/music.py info music/<file>`).\n   When `job.json` has `\"epidemicSound\": true`, the brand's Epidemic Sound account is also an option: `epidemic-sound.md`\n   says when to use it and how to bring a track from it into `music/`.\n   Then `python3 $SK/beatfit.py footage work/edl/<id>.tsv music/<file> auto <seconds> work/edl/<id>.beat.tsv`\n   re-times the same shots onto the beat and prints `audio_start` and `video_seconds`. From here on use the\n   `.beat.tsv` as the cut's EDL and `video_seconds` as its `seconds`. `auto` starts at the loudest stretch (the\n   chorus or drop); give a number instead to start elsewhere.\n6. **Render**: first, when any shot keeps its sound, `python3 $SK/speechcheck.py work/edl/<id>.tsv work/listen`\n   (the `.beat.tsv` with music). It must end in `PASS`. A `FIX` line means a shot cuts into a word or stops\n   mid-sentence, and gives the corrected EDL line: put that line in the EDL, fit again, and run it again. Never\n   render a cut it fails. `FAIL no transcript` means the clips were never listened to in this run: run\n   `python3 $SK/listen.py footage work/listen` and then the check again.\n   Then `bash $SK/render.sh footage work/edl/<id>.tsv out/previews/<id>.mp4` (the `.beat.tsv` with music).\n   With music, then `bash $SK/mix.sh out/previews/<id>.mp4 music/<file> <audio_start>` (sets loudness and a short\n   fade out). After every `mix.sh`, `python3 $SK/soundcheck.py out/previews/<id>.mp4` measures the result: how\n   loud the cut is, that the music sits well under anyone speaking, and that the music fades out at the end. It\n   must end in `PASS`; a `FIX` line says what to run instead (for music too loud under a voice, `mix.sh` again\n   with the number it gives as a fourth argument). If it still fails after two tries, keep the cut and say so in\n   your one-line finish. Read\n   `out/previews/<id>.check.jpg`: every shot upright, filling the frame, not black. Then read every\n   `out/previews/<id>.review-N.jpg`: five frames across each shot, one shot per row. In every row something must\n   visibly happen from the first frame to the last (under the profile: the hit, the rep, the pour, the reaction);\n   a row where the frames look alike, or people only walk or wait, is a wasted shot: replace it from another\n   `moments.py` window, fit and render again. Do 15s first, then 30s.\n   When a shot keeps its sound, `render.sh` says so and `mix.sh` lowers the music under it by itself. With no\n   music but a shot that keeps its sound: `bash $SK/mix.sh out/previews/<id>.mp4 none 0`, then `soundcheck.py`\n   as above.\n7. **Words**: for each cut, ~10 `hookOptions` per `hooks.md` and a `caption` per `captions.md`. Suggest `coverAt`:\n   seconds into the cut of the best cover frame (the finished result, no motion blur).\n8. **Review**: for each cut, ask the `reviewer` subagent to check it (one call per cut). Tell it the cut's `id`\n   and paste the cut's `hookOptions` and `caption` into the message. It answers `VERDICT: pass` or a short list of\n   fixes. Apply every fix (re-fit, re-render, re-mix, re-check, or rewrite the words) unless it contradicts the\n   request. If it found a shot where nothing happens or a request that was not followed, ask it once more after\n   fixing; two reviews at most. If the reviewer cannot be reached, carry on without it.\n9. **Manifest**: write `out/manifest.json` (below). `python3 $SK/edl2json.py json work/edl/<id>.tsv` prints each\n   cut's `edl` array exactly as rendered; paste it in unchanged. Then finish with one short line.\n\n## Sound from the clips\n\nA shot is silent under the music unless its EDL line ends in `|1`. Keep a shot's sound when:\n- `listen.py` marks the clip `TALKING`, or the person says they want to hear someone (\"open with him talking\",\n  \"I can't hear her\", \"keep what he says\"). A talking clip that is used is used for its words: take whole\n  phrases by the times in `work/listen/speech.tsv` (start 0.2s before the first word, end 0.3s after the last),\n  never the middle of a sentence, and let the shot run as long as the phrase needs (3 to 8 seconds is normal).\n  `speechcheck.py` checks this word by word before every render: a shot that clips a word or stops mid-sentence\n  is not a judgement call.\n  `fit.py` and `beatfit.py` leave these shots' words whole and do not count them towards pacing.\n- The person asks for the real sound of something (the hits, the crowd).\nUse the strongest one or two phrases, not everything said. On a first cut, a `TALKING` clip opens the cut or\ncloses it unless the brief says otherwise; never drop it in as a silent one-second shot. If the words are needed\nbut `listen.py` found none or could not run, still keep the sound on that shot and say so in the reply.\nNever tell the person the voice cannot be kept.\n\n## Revisions\n\nRead `previous.json`, the request and `history`. Change only what was asked; everything else stays exactly as it\nwas, and everything asked earlier in `history` still holds (longer shots stay long, the opener they chose stays).\n- A cut that does not change: copy its manifest entry unchanged, including `preview` `{\"key\": ...}`.\n- A cut that changes: `python3 $SK/edl2json.py tsv previous.json <id> work/edl/<id>.prev.tsv`, edit that EDL as\n  asked (keep every shot the request does not touch), then fit (only if the length must change, with the same\n  pace as before: `held` if the shots were long or `history` asked for it), render, check\n  and review as above, and give the cut `preview` `{\"path\": ...}`. Keep its `id`. New words only if the request is about words.\n- A new cut asked for: make it as in a first cut, with a new `id`.\n- \"Change the song\": start from the previous EDL, pick a different track (from `music/`, or from Epidemic Sound\n  per `epidemic-sound.md` when it is connected), beatfit, render, mix. \"No music\":\n  render the previous EDL as is, no mix, `music` null. Both count as a cut that changes.\nIf the request is unclear about which cut it means, apply it to the cut it most plausibly means and say so in the reply.\n\n## out/manifest.json\n\n```json\n{\n  \"reply\": \"Two cuts are ready: a 15s and a 30s, both opening on the finished stand. ...\",\n  \"question\": null,\n  \"cuts\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"reel-15\", \"title\": \"Stand 15s\", \"seconds\": 15, \"mode\": \"music\",\n      \"preview\": {\"path\": \"out/previews/reel-15.mp4\"},\n      \"edl\": [{\"file\": \"IMG_0412.mov\", \"in\": 3.2, \"dur\": 1.1, \"speed\": 1, \"cropX\": null},\n              {\"file\": \"talk.mov\", \"in\": 0.8, \"dur\": 5.1, \"speed\": 1, \"cropX\": null, \"sound\": true}],\n      \"hookOptions\": [\"...\", \"...\"], \"caption\": \"...\", \"script\": null, \"coverAt\": 13.5,\n      \"music\": {\"file\": \"03-summer-days.mp3\", \"start\": 41.218}\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- `mode` is always `\"music\"` and `script` always `null` (no voice-over yet). `seconds` is the cut's length.\n- `music`: the track's `file` and the `audio_start` the preview was mixed from, or `null` for a cut with no music.\n- `\"sound\": true` marks a shot that keeps its clip's own sound; `edl2json.py` writes it for you.\n- Ids: lowercase with dashes (`reel-15`, `reel-30`, `story-15`). Titles are plain (\"Stand 30s\").\n- `reply`: what goes back into the conversation, in the owner's plain voice: two or three short sentences on what\n  you made and any choice they might want to change (an odd clip left out, a segment with only one angle). No lists\n  of files, no technical terms, no praise of your own work.\n- `question`: `null`, or ONE short question when something essential is missing (see `intake.md`). With a\n  question you may still deliver cuts; if you truly cannot make anything, `cuts` is `[]`.\n- Keep all text plain: no markdown, no emojis except in `caption`.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Taking a one-second slice of a pan. Use the whole pan sped up.\n- Choosing a shot from a single frame on a sheet. Look at the stretch closely first (`sample.sh` with `clip|from|to`).\n- Muting someone who is talking to the camera, or cutting them off mid-sentence.\n- Going back to quick cuts on a later revision after the person asked for longer shots.\n- Trusting a clip's reported frame rate or length without `scan.py`.\n- An `edl` in the manifest that differs from the EDL the preview was rendered from. Always use `edl2json.py`.\n- Inventing facts in hooks or captions. Only the brief and what the footage shows.\n",
  "captions.md": "# Post captions\n\nWrite each cut's `caption` from three sources only: `request` and `instructions` in `job.json`, `brand.voice`, and what\nthe footage shows. Do not invent places, names, clients, dates, prices or \"part N\" claims. A fact you do not have is\nleft out, never guessed.\n\n- Rewrite the person's notes into natural, flowing copy (never their words verbatim, never choppy fragments), simple\n  and not cheesy, in the brand's voice.\n- A few relevant emojis, then 3 hand-picked hashtags on their own line at the end.\n- One story per job: the longest cut gets the fuller caption (2 to 3 sentences); shorter cuts get a shorter version\n  with the same hashtags. A story (`videoKind` story) gets `\"caption\": \"\"`: Instagram stories have no caption.\n- If the request mentions a series or what viewers should do next (\"part 1, follow for the setup\"), say it once, plainly.\n\n## Example of the voice (approved, for tone only; do not reuse the wording or facts)\n\nNotes were: \"we are taking over bloomingdales costa mesa with a fall activation. here is the build process.. this is\npart 1 follow to see the actual set up or something\". A first draft followed the notes too literally and read as choppy.\n\n> Bloomingdale's Costa Mesa is getting a fall takeover 🍂 Here's how the hot apple cider stand came together, from a pile of boxes to the finished build 🔨 This is part 1, so follow along to see the full setup 🍎\n>\n> #bloomingdales #fallactivation #buildprocess\n",
  "epidemic-sound.md": "# Epidemic Sound (an option, only when connected)\n\nUse this only when `job.json` has `\"epidemicSound\": true`: the brand has connected its own Epidemic Sound\naccount, so you can search that catalogue and bring a track into `music/` for this job. When it is false or\nmissing, the two tools below do not exist; pick from `music/` as usual and never mention Epidemic Sound.\n\nIt is an extra source, not a replacement. Tracks already in `music/` are the brand's own library and are the\ndefault.\n\n## When to use it\n\n- The request or instructions ask for it: \"use Epidemic Sound\", \"find a new song\", \"something we haven't used\".\n- The request or instructions name a mood, genre or tempo that no track in `job.json` `music` fits.\n- \"Change the song\" on a revision, when nothing else in `music/` fits what they asked for.\n- Music is wanted and `job.json` lists no tracks.\n\nOtherwise use `music/`.\n\n## How\n\n1. **Search**: call `mcp__epidemic__search_music` with `term`, a few plain words for the feel\n   (\"upbeat tropical house\", \"warm acoustic morning\", \"driving hip hop instrumental\"). Narrow with `bpmMin` /\n   `bpmMax` when the pace matters, and `vocals: false` unless they asked for a song with singing. Each result has\n   `id`, `title`, `artist`, `bpm`, `seconds` and `tags`. A job gets 12 searches, so make each one count: change the\n   words rather than repeating a search.\n2. **Choose** one track per cut by tags and tempo against the brief and the footage's pace. For 15s and 30s cuts a\n   clear, steady beat matters more than anything else. The same track can serve both cuts.\n3. **Download**: call `mcp__epidemic__download_track` with that `id`. It saves the track into `music/` and answers\n   with its `file` (for example `music/es-summer-knot-b93f23e5.mp3`). A job gets 4 downloads: decide first,\n   then download.\n4. **Use it like any other track**: `python3 $SK/music.py info music/<file>` for its beat and loudest stretch,\n   then `beatfit.py`, `render.sh` and `mix.sh` exactly as in SKILL.md. In the manifest the cut's `music` is\n   `{\"file\": \"<file>\", \"start\": <audio_start>}` as usual. The track is added to the brand's library when the cut\n   is delivered.\n5. **Say so** in `reply`, in one plain clause: the track's title and that it came from Epidemic Sound.\n\n## If a tool answers with an error\n\nTry once more only if the message suggests it (a different search, for example). Otherwise fall back to a track\nfrom `music/`, or a silent cut when there are none, and say in `reply` that Epidemic Sound was not available. Never\nask a question about it and never leave a cut unfinished because of it.\n",
  "hooks-formulas.md": "<!-- Source: github.com/rediumvex/viral-hooks-skill (MIT, Copyright its authors), hooks-database.md, copied 2026-11-13. Reference only: read hooks.md for how to use it. -->\n\n# Top 100 Viral Hook Formulas\n\nBattle-tested hook structures for short-form video, posts, threads, and headlines. Organized by 10 psychology triggers.\n\n---\n\n## Category 01 — Curiosity Gap\n\n**Why it works:** The brain treats unanswered questions like open browser tabs. It can't relax until the gap closes — so the viewer keeps watching.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 001 | \"Nobody talks about [unexpected thing] in [niche]\" | \"Nobody talks about why your Reels flop after 3am posts\" | All |\n| 002 | \"I found out why [common problem] keeps happening\" | \"I found out why your followers never convert\" | All |\n| 003 | \"The [niche] industry doesn't want you to know this\" | \"The skincare industry doesn't want you to know this\" | TikTok / IG |\n| 004 | \"What happens when you [unexpected action] for [timeframe]\" | \"What happens when you post 3x a day for 90 days\" | YouTube |\n| 005 | \"I've been [doing thing] wrong my entire life\" | \"I've been writing captions wrong my entire life\" | All |\n| 006 | \"The truth about [topic] that nobody is saying\" | \"The truth about freelancing that nobody is saying\" | All |\n| 007 | \"Why [common belief] is actually the opposite\" | \"Why 'eat less, move more' is actually making you fat\" | All |\n| 008 | \"I wasn't supposed to share this, but…\" | \"I wasn't supposed to share this, but here's what big agencies charge\" | TikTok / IG |\n| 009 | \"What I learned from [unusual source]\" | \"What I learned from a 70-year-old stripper about negotiation\" | All |\n| 010 | \"The hidden cost of [popular thing]\" | \"The hidden cost of going remote that nobody mentions\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Category 02 — Contrarian / Pattern-Interrupt\n\n**Why it works:** Contradicting a belief creates cognitive dissonance. People engage to resolve it — agree or argue, both feed the algorithm.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 011 | \"Stop [common advice everyone gives]\" | \"Stop posting every day — here's what actually grows\" | All |\n| 012 | \"[Popular strategy] is dead. Here's what replaced it\" | \"Hashtag research is dead. Here's what replaced it\" | All |\n| 013 | \"Unpopular opinion: [bold claim about niche]\" | \"Unpopular opinion: Canva templates are killing your brand\" | LinkedIn / X |\n| 014 | \"I quit [thing everyone does] and [positive result]\" | \"I quit posting Reels and my engagement doubled\" | All |\n| 015 | \"Everyone's doing [trend] but they're missing [key detail]\" | \"Everyone's doing carousels but missing the last slide\" | IG / LinkedIn |\n| 016 | \"[Common advice] is making things worse\" | \"More cardio is making your body fat worse\" | All |\n| 017 | \"Hot take: [contrarian belief]\" | \"Hot take: most personal brands are just glorified resumes\" | X / LinkedIn |\n| 018 | \"I'm done with [popular practice]\" | \"I'm done with growth hacking. Here's what I do now\" | All |\n| 019 | \"If you're still doing [X], stop\" | \"If you're still cold-emailing the same way, stop\" | All |\n| 020 | \"The opposite of [common advice] worked for me\" | \"The opposite of 'hustle' grew my business 5x\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Category 03 — Authority & Proof\n\n**Why it works:** Specific numbers and proof activate the credibility filter. Vague claims get ignored. Precise claims earn attention.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 021 | \"I [impressive result] in [short timeframe]. Here's the breakdown\" | \"I grew to 100K followers in 6 months. Here's the breakdown\" | All |\n| 022 | \"After analyzing [N] [things], here's what works\" | \"After analyzing 500 viral posts, here's what works\" | All |\n| 023 | \"[N] years of [experience] taught me one lesson\" | \"8 years of freelancing taught me one brutal lesson\" | LinkedIn |\n| 024 | \"I've helped [N] [people] do [result]. The #1 mistake is…\" | \"I've helped 200 creators monetize. The #1 mistake is…\" | All |\n| 025 | \"My [metric] went from [X] to [Y] when I changed [one thing]\" | \"My CTR went from 2% to 11% when I changed thumbnails\" | YouTube |\n| 026 | \"I tried [thing] for [N] days. Here's what happened\" | \"I tried cold showers for 30 days. Here's what happened\" | YouTube |\n| 027 | \"[N] of my [content/clients] all do this one thing\" | \"All 12 of my 6-figure clients do this one thing\" | All |\n| 028 | \"After [N] failures, I finally figured out [X]\" | \"After 47 failed launches, I finally figured out pricing\" | LinkedIn |\n| 029 | \"Here's the data nobody is sharing about [topic]\" | \"Here's the data nobody is sharing about LinkedIn reach\" | LinkedIn / X |\n| 030 | \"I tested [N] different [things]. Only one worked\" | \"I tested 13 different cold-email subject lines. One worked\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Category 04 — Emotional Trigger\n\n**Why it works:** Content that mirrors internal dialogue feels personal. The viewer thinks \"this was made for me.\" That's the most powerful hook of all.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 031 | \"If you're struggling with [pain point], read this\" | \"If you're struggling to get past 1K followers, read this\" | All |\n| 032 | \"This is for the [type of person] who [relatable struggle]\" | \"This is for the creator who posts daily and gets crickets\" | All |\n| 033 | \"I almost gave up on [thing] until [turning point]\" | \"I almost gave up on YouTube until I found this\" | YouTube |\n| 034 | \"The hard truth about [aspirational thing] nobody tells you\" | \"The hard truth about going viral nobody tells you\" | All |\n| 035 | \"You're not [negative self-belief] — you just need [solution]\" | \"You're not bad at marketing — you just need better hooks\" | All |\n| 036 | \"If this hits home, you're not alone\" | \"If this hits home, you're not the only founder feeling lost\" | All |\n| 037 | \"I cried writing this…\" | \"I cried writing this — but it needs to be said\" | LinkedIn / IG |\n| 038 | \"To the person who needs to hear this…\" | \"To the founder who's about to quit — read this first\" | All |\n| 039 | \"I see you. Here's what I wish I knew\" | \"I see you, struggling first-year freelancer. Here's what I wish I knew\" | All |\n| 040 | \"If you've ever felt [emotion], watch this\" | \"If you've ever felt invisible at work, watch this\" | TikTok / IG |\n\n---\n\n## Category 05 — Listicle & Value Stack\n\n**Why it works:** Numbers set expectations. Viewers calculate value-to-time before committing. \"Save this\" / \"bookmark\" = micro-CTA boosting engagement signal.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 041 | \"[N] [things] that will [outcome] (save this)\" | \"7 free tools that will 10x your content (save this)\" | All |\n| 042 | \"The only [N] [things] you need to [achieve result]\" | \"The only 3 apps you need to run a faceless YouTube channel\" | All |\n| 043 | \"I spent [time/money] on [thing] so you don't have to\" | \"I spent $2,000 on YouTube courses so you don't have to\" | YouTube / IG |\n| 044 | \"[Thing] cheat sheet (bookmark this)\" | \"Instagram algorithm cheat sheet (bookmark this)\" | All |\n| 045 | \"[N] things I wish I knew at [age/stage]\" | \"7 things I wish I knew at 25 about money\" | All |\n| 046 | \"[N] tools every [profession/role] needs\" | \"5 tools every solo founder needs in 2026\" | All |\n| 047 | \"[N] habits that changed my life in [timeframe]\" | \"4 habits that changed my life in 90 days\" | All |\n| 048 | \"[N] mistakes I made so you can skip them\" | \"6 mistakes I made building my agency so you can skip them\" | LinkedIn |\n| 049 | \"The ultimate [topic] starter pack\" | \"The ultimate freelance writer starter pack\" | X / IG |\n| 050 | \"[N] underrated [tools/habits/strategies]\" | \"5 underrated AI tools that actually save me hours\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Category 06 — Question Hooks\n\n**Why it works:** Direct address forces the brain to formulate an answer. The viewer enters dialogue mode, can't stay passive.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 051 | \"Why does [common annoyance] keep happening?\" | \"Why does my plant keep dying even though I water it?\" | All |\n| 052 | \"What if [shocking premise]?\" | \"What if everything you know about productivity is wrong?\" | All |\n| 053 | \"Have you ever wondered why [common thing]?\" | \"Have you ever wondered why airline food tastes so bad?\" | YouTube |\n| 054 | \"Are you making this [niche] mistake?\" | \"Are you making this LinkedIn profile mistake?\" | LinkedIn |\n| 055 | \"Is this you?\" | \"Is this you at 4pm every workday?\" | TikTok / IG |\n| 056 | \"Can you guess [shocking number/fact]?\" | \"Can you guess how much I made my first year freelancing?\" | All |\n| 057 | \"What would you do if [scenario]?\" | \"What would you do if your biggest client fired you tomorrow?\" | LinkedIn |\n| 058 | \"How long until [inevitable outcome]?\" | \"How long until AI replaces your entry-level marketing job?\" | LinkedIn / X |\n| 059 | \"Did your [authority figure] ever tell you about [X]?\" | \"Did your school ever tell you how 401(k) fees work?\" | All |\n| 060 | \"Why is nobody talking about [X]?\" | \"Why is nobody talking about the 4-day work week data?\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Category 07 — Story & Narrative\n\n**Why it works:** Mid-story openings hijack the brain's narrative-completion drive. The viewer needs the resolution.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 061 | \"Last week, [unexpected thing] happened\" | \"Last week, a stranger sent me $10,000\" | All |\n| 062 | \"3 years ago I was [low point]. Today [high point]\" | \"3 years ago I was sleeping in my car. Today I just bought my parents a house\" | All |\n| 063 | \"I almost made the worst mistake of my [career/life]\" | \"I almost made the worst hiring mistake of my career\" | LinkedIn |\n| 064 | \"Yesterday a stranger said something that…\" | \"Yesterday a stranger said something that changed how I see freelancing\" | All |\n| 065 | \"I just got [shocking message/email]\" | \"I just got an email that made me question everything\" | All |\n| 066 | \"The day I realized [insight]…\" | \"The day I realized I was the bottleneck in my own business\" | LinkedIn |\n| 067 | \"I never thought I'd say this, but…\" | \"I never thought I'd say this, but I'm leaving Twitter\" | All |\n| 068 | \"POV: you're [scenario]\" | \"POV: you're a 32yo woman trying to lose 5 pounds\" | TikTok / IG |\n| 069 | \"When [character] said [quote], I knew…\" | \"When my mentor said 'you're avoiding the work,' I knew he was right\" | LinkedIn / X |\n| 070 | \"Here's a story that will change how you see [topic]\" | \"Here's a story that will change how you see customer service\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Category 08 — Negation / Myth Buster\n\n**Why it works:** Two-word negations stop the scroll in 0.3 seconds. They contradict assumed reality and demand explanation.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 071 | \"Not [common belief].\" | \"Not arthritis.\" / \"Not a heart attack.\" / \"Not anxiety.\" | TikTok / IG |\n| 072 | \"[Common belief] is a lie.\" | \"'Find your passion' is a lie.\" | All |\n| 073 | \"It's not [common cause] — it's [real cause]\" | \"It's not lack of motivation — it's poor sleep\" | All |\n| 074 | \"Forget what they told you about [topic]\" | \"Forget what they told you about cold pitching\" | All |\n| 075 | \"[Popular thing] doesn't work. Here's why\" | \"Affirmations don't work. Here's why\" | All |\n| 076 | \"Stop blaming [usual suspect]. The real issue is…\" | \"Stop blaming the algorithm. The real issue is your hook\" | All |\n| 077 | \"There's no such thing as [popular concept]\" | \"There's no such thing as 'work-life balance'\" | LinkedIn |\n| 078 | \"[Big claim from authority] is wrong\" | \"What your doctor said about cholesterol is wrong\" | All |\n| 079 | \"Myth: [popular belief]. Reality: [contrarian truth]\" | \"Myth: drink 8 glasses of water. Reality: you don't need to\" | All |\n| 080 | \"If you still believe [X], read this\" | \"If you still believe 'follow your dreams,' read this\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Category 09 — Specificity & Data\n\n**Why it works:** Specific numbers, times, and amounts feel real. Vague hooks feel made up. Specificity = credibility.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 081 | \"[Specific number]: that's how many [thing]\" | \"$847: that's what I waste on subscriptions monthly\" | All |\n| 082 | \"[Specific time]: the exact moment [thing happens]\" | \"3:07am: the exact moment cortisol wakes you in peri\" | All |\n| 083 | \"$[amount]: what [common thing] really costs\" | \"$23,000: what one bad hire really costs you\" | LinkedIn |\n| 084 | \"[N]%: the share of people who [X]\" | \"73%: the share of LinkedIn posts that get under 100 views\" | LinkedIn |\n| 085 | \"[Specific date]: when everything changed\" | \"March 14, 2024: the day I deleted Instagram\" | All |\n| 086 | \"[Number]:[number]: the ratio that matters\" | \"3:1: the talk-to-listen ratio that closes deals\" | LinkedIn |\n| 087 | \"[Lab number/metric]: the threshold nobody mentions\" | \"Ferritin: 70. The threshold nobody mentions\" | All |\n| 088 | \"[Time of day]: the exact window for [X]\" | \"6-8am: the exact window cortisol wants you outside\" | All |\n| 089 | \"[Specific dose/amount]: what actually works\" | \"30 grams: the protein floor at breakfast\" | All |\n| 090 | \"[Age]: when [X] starts and nobody warns you\" | \"35: when perimenopause starts and nobody warns you\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Category 10 — Personal Confession\n\n**Why it works:** Vulnerability is rare and rewarded. Real confession from a real person beats polished expert content for trust + share rate.\n\n| ID | Formula | Example | Platform |\n|----|---------|---------|----------|\n| 091 | \"I'm about to share something embarrassing\" | \"I'm about to share something embarrassing about my first year freelancing\" | All |\n| 092 | \"I'll admit it: I [confession]\" | \"I'll admit it: I bought followers in 2019\" | All |\n| 093 | \"Nobody talks about this part of [experience]\" | \"Nobody talks about the loneliness part of running a business\" | LinkedIn / IG |\n| 094 | \"I failed publicly so you can succeed privately\" | \"I failed publicly so you can build your agency without my mistakes\" | LinkedIn |\n| 095 | \"Here's what I lied about for years\" | \"Here's what I lied about on every podcast — until now\" | All |\n| 096 | \"The thing I was most afraid to say\" | \"The thing I was most afraid to say about my industry\" | LinkedIn |\n| 097 | \"I'm tired of pretending [X]\" | \"I'm tired of pretending hustle culture is healthy\" | LinkedIn |\n| 098 | \"Real talk: [vulnerable admission]\" | \"Real talk: I made $0 for 14 months before my first $10K month\" | All |\n| 099 | \"I never thought I'd post this\" | \"I never thought I'd post this — but my biggest deal taught me a brutal lesson\" | LinkedIn |\n| 100 | \"Behind the scenes of [aspirational thing]\" | \"Behind the scenes of my 6-figure month — including the panic attack\" | All |\n\n---\n\n## Workflow tip\n\nThe two best hooks per video are usually from **different categories**. Mix one stop-scroll hook (negation, specificity, question) with one retention hook (story, confession, emotional). The first stops the thumb. The second keeps them watching past 3 seconds.\n\n---\n\n*Adapted from [social-media-hooks-database](https://github.com/jakeolschewski/social-media-hooks-database) (MIT) plus 75+ patterns curated from viral short-form content across TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, LinkedIn, and X.*\n",
  "hooks.md": "# Hooks: opening words on screen\n\nEvery cut gets ~10 `hookOptions`: drafts of the opening on-screen text. The person picks one, writes their own, or\nchooses none in the app; nothing is burned into the preview. Method adapted from `artemnovitckii/content-skills`\n(viral-hooks, MIT); formulas in `hooks-formulas.md` are from `rediumvex/viral-hooks-skill` (MIT).\n\n## What a hook has to do\n\nIn the first two seconds the viewer must know what the video is about and have one open question pulling them on.\nClear but flat fails. Clever but unclear fails.\n\n## Four ways a hook fails (check every draft)\n\n1. **Delay**: the topic arrives late. Cut everything before it. No greeting, no \"so\", no \"wait till you see\".\n2. **Confusion**: it needs a second read. Short plain words, active voice, one idea.\n3. **Irrelevance**: clear, but not about the viewer. Prefer \"you/your\" or a pain they already have. An \"I\" or \"we\"\n   hook is fine for a story or confession when the viewer would want the same result or fear the same mistake.\n4. **Disinterest**: nothing left open. Put what people assume (A) against what happened (B). The believable gap\n   between them is the curiosity.\n\n## Writing the ~10 `hookOptions`\n\n1. Read `brand.voice`, `instructions` and `request` in `job.json`. Match the voice; reuse none of its wording verbatim.\n2. Use only facts the brief gives or the footage plainly shows: real numbers, real days, real places, real client.\n   Never invent a number, a result, a place, a name or a struggle. If the strongest hook needs a fact you do not\n   have, write a different hook.\n3. Draft from at least five different categories in `hooks-formulas.md`; do not hand over one idea reworded ten\n   times. Treat the formulas as structures, never paste the wording. Fits for build and event videos: 004, 026\n   (time-boxed), 043, 048 (cost and mistakes), 056 (guess the number), 062 (before and after), 073 (it's not A, it's\n   B), 081, 083 (a specific number first), 100 (behind the scenes). Skip the LinkedIn-style and \"industry doesn't\n   want you to know\" ones.\n4. On-screen text is 3 to 9 words, readable in under two seconds, at most two lines.\n5. It has to be true to what the video delivers. A hook the cut does not pay off loses the viewer at the reveal.\n6. Revisions: keep the previous `hookOptions` unless the request is about the words.\n\n## Not allowed\n\n\"Wait till you see this\", \"you won't believe\", \"nobody talks about this\" with nothing named, \"POV:\" or \"Story\ntime:\" as empty wrappers, a call to action as the opener, shock the footage does not back up.\n",
  "intake.md": "# Intake (non-interactive)\n\nDecide from `job.json` and the scan. Take anything the `request` or `instructions` say over these defaults.\n\n1. **Project type**: build (default), event recap or training. Event recap only when the request says event,\n   recap, party, launch, activation day or similar, or the footage plainly shows an event with no build. Training\n   when the footage shows sport, a workout, sparring, a class, a game or any physical practice.\n   Build uses `profiles/build.md`; an event recap uses `profiles/event.md`; training uses `profiles/training.md`.\n   Build and event, in short:\n\n   | | Build | Event recap |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | Order | Strictly chronological | Loose; best moments can move |\n   | Hook | The finished result | The peak moment |\n   | Timelapse | Heavy, it shows progress | Light, for setup and crowd |\n   | Ranks highest | Visible change, the reveal | People, reactions, atmosphere |\n\n2. **Segments**: group the footage by content and shooting order (filenames from phones usually sort by time).\n   Use them for chronological order and to spread screen time; v1 makes whole-project cuts only.\n\n3. **Lengths**: `reel` = one 15s cut and one 30s cut. `story` = one 15s cut. Other lengths or counts only when\n   the request asks (at most 4 cuts, each 5 to 90 s).\n\n4. **Mode**: music-led pacing with a track from `music/`, and no added voice-over. Clips where someone talks keep\n   their words (SKILL.md, \"Sound from the clips\"): the person filmed them to be heard.\n\n5. **Odd clips**: clips `scan.py` flags as horizontal or a different shape are cropped to fill (choose `crop_x` to\n   keep the action in frame) when they are strong; otherwise leave them out. Say so in the reply if a strong clip\n   was left out.\n\n6. **The story, for hooks and captions**: only from `request`, `instructions`, `brand.voice` and what the footage\n   shows. Missing facts are left out of the words, not invented.\n\n## When to ask (rarely)\n\nReturn a `question` only when the cut cannot be made well without the answer, for example: the request names a\nmoment or product that no clip shows; or the footage is two unrelated shoots and the request does not say which.\nAsk one short question with your recommendation in it (\"I can only find the setup, not the opening night. Cut the\nsetup only?\"). If you can still make a sensible cut, make it and ask in the same manifest.\nA missing caption fact is never a reason to ask: write the caption without it.\n\n**The story**: on a first cut, when the request and instructions say nothing about what this is or why it matters\n(just \"make a reel\" or nothing at all), still make the cuts, and ask one short question about the story in the same\nmanifest, with your best guess in it (\"Looks like the opening of the cider stand at Bloomingdale's. What should people\ntake away: the build, the turnout, or come visit?\"). Never on a revision, and never when the brief already says.\n",
  "profiles/build.md": "# Build profile\n\nFor footage of something being made or assembled, with no talking to camera.\n\n## What makes a moment worth using (strongest first)\n\n1. **Change you can see**: a piece placed, a panel lifted, a line drawn.\n2. **Reveal**: the finished thing, or a step back that shows scale.\n3. **Hands and tools in action**: close and purposeful.\n4. **People reacting**: a smile, a look at the result.\n5. **Texture and detail**: close-ups of material or finish, used as breathers.\n\nCounts against a moment: camera shake or refocusing, someone blocking the shot, nothing happening,\nand the first and last second of a clip (phone being raised or lowered).\n\n## Structure\n\n- **Hook**: the first one to two seconds is the strongest visual in the cut, usually the finished result.\n- **Order**: chronological after the hook; end on the finished result from a different moment or angle.\n- **Before/after pairs**: the same subject from a similar position at different stages. Flag them in the scan and cut them together.\n- **Variety**: no source range used twice in a cut (`fit.py` enforces this); alternate wide and close.\n- **Long takes**: use both real-speed moments and timelapse stretches from the same clip. Split a long\n  build take into consecutive timelapse stages with real-speed close-ups between them.\n\n## Pacing\n\n| Cut length | Shot length | Timelapse share |\n|---|---|---|\n| 15s | about 1s (0.8 to 1.6s) | a few shots |\n| 30s | 1.5 to 2s | about a third |\n| 45s | 2 to 3s | about half |\n| 60s | 2.5 to 3.5s | half or more |\n\nTimelapse speeds that worked: 4x to 8x for pans, 30x to 100x for assembly stretches, up to 500x for takes over 10 minutes.\n\n## Rules learned from review\n\n- **Pan rule**: when a clip only shows its subject by panning across it, use the whole pan sped up, never a slice.\n  A one-second slice of a twelve-second pan read as \"a random box\".\n- **Same camera position repeated** (for example a locked-off wide of a room) gets monotonous. Break it with\n  real-speed moments, and say so in the reply when a segment has no other angles.\n- **Crop for clips that are not 9:16**: set `crop_x` (0 = left, 0.5 = centre, 1 = right) to keep the action,\n  such as the pen in a lettering close-up, in frame.\n",
  "profiles/event.md": "# Event profile\n\nFor footage of an event, activation or pop-up: loading in, setting up, the day itself, people. Use it when\n`intake.md` says the project is an event recap. Pacing is the same as `build.md` (the table under \"Pacing\").\n\n## What makes a moment worth using (strongest first)\n\n1. **People in the moment**: a guest taking the first sip, a laugh, a hand reaching for the product.\n2. **The peak**: the busiest, liveliest stretch; a crowd at the stand, a line forming.\n3. **Product in action**: pouring, serving, handing over, a close-up of the thing being enjoyed.\n4. **The finished setup**: the stand or space ready before doors open, a step back that shows scale.\n5. **Getting there**: loading the truck, carrying pieces in, the night-before setup. A few beats, not the story.\n6. **Details**: signage, branding, textures, used as one-shot breathers.\n\nCounts against a moment: camera shake or refocusing, people walking through the frame, empty space where the\ncrowd should be, nothing happening, and the first and last second of a clip (phone being raised or lowered).\n\n## Choosing the part of a clip\n\nNever take a clip from its start by default. Sample it, find the frame where the moment happens (the sip, the\npour, the reaction), and start the shot just before it so the action lands inside the shot.\n\n- **Real speed** for anything with people: reactions, serving, conversation, crowd energy. Speeding up people\n  looks wrong. Keep these short (about 1s in a 15s cut) and cut on the action.\n- **Sped up** only for: a pan across the setup (use the whole pan, 4x to 8x), loading or setup stretches\n  (timelapse, 8x to 30x), and long locked-off crowd shots (8x to 16x).\n- In a 15s cut, two or three sped-up shots at most. The rest are real-speed snippets.\n\n## Structure\n\n- **Hook**: the first one to two seconds is the peak moment or the most striking image (the finished stand with\n  people at it), never loading in.\n- **Order**: loose. Getting there (brief) -> finished setup -> the event building to its busiest -> a closing image\n  (the setup at its best, or a last reaction). Best moments can move.\n- **Variety**: alternate wide (the crowd, the setup) and close (hands, faces, product). No source range used twice.\n- **Segments**: footage in groups (truck load, night before, event day) gets screen time by how good it is, not\n  evenly. Event day usually earns most of it.\n",
  "profiles/training.md": "# Training profile\n\nFor footage of sport or physical practice: a workout, sparring, pad work, a class, a game, a run. Often one or\ntwo long takes from a phone on a tripod, where most of the take is waiting and a few seconds are the reason it\nwas filmed. The job is to find those seconds.\n\n## What makes a moment worth using (strongest first)\n\n1. **Clean contact**: a kick, punch, knee or elbow landing; a ball struck; a lift locked out; a takedown.\n2. **Combinations and exchanges**: two or more actions in a row, attack and answer, a full rep or set at speed.\n3. **Power and speed you can see**: the pad jumping, a body moved by a hit, an explosive start, a full extension.\n4. **Skill details**: footwork into a strike, a check, a slip, a clean technique close to the camera.\n5. **The person and the room**: a coach talking, a glove touch, a breath between rounds, a laugh. One or two, as\n   breathers, or to open or close.\n\nCounts against a moment: walking, circling with nothing thrown, resetting, adjusting gear, waiting for a partner,\nbacks to the camera, the action hidden behind someone, and the first and last seconds of a clip.\n\n## Finding the moments\n\n- Use `moments.py`: its strongest windows are where limbs move fastest and pads or bags are hit hardest. Look at\n  every window you might use with `sample.sh` (`clip|from|to`), and take the shot only when the frames show the\n  strike or rep itself. Start about half a second before the action and end just after it lands or finishes, so\n  the whole movement is in the shot. Never cut away in the middle of a combination.\n- Spread shots across the take (early, middle and late), unless one stretch is clearly the best.\n- When the person is in the footage, favour the moments where they are the one doing the action.\n\n## Pacing\n\nUse `held` with `fit.py`. A strike needs its wind-up and its landing: shots run 2 to 4 seconds, a long\ncombination or exchange up to 6. A 15s cut is 4 to 6 shots; a 30s cut is 8 to 12. Do not chop an exchange into\none-second pieces, and do not speed people up.\n\n## Structure\n\n- **Hook**: the single best hit or exchange, or the coach or athlete talking when a clip was filmed for that\n  (keep its sound; see SKILL.md, \"Sound from the clips\").\n- **Order**: loose. Build from technique to the hardest exchanges; end on the strongest hit or a clear finish\n  (a glove touch, a last rep, a breath).\n- **One camera position** is normal here. Keep it alive by changing what happens (kicks, hands, clinch, defence),\n  and crop in (`crop_x`) for one or two shots when the frame allows it.\n",
  "review.md": "# Review: a second pair of eyes on each cut\n\nYou are the reviewer. The editor made a cut and asked you to check it before it goes to the person. You did not\nmake it, so judge only what is in front of you. Be quick and specific. Never edit files; only read and answer.\n\n## What to read\n\n- `job.json`: `request` (what the person asked for), `history` (what they asked earlier in the same conversation;\n  it still stands unless a later message changes it), `instructions`, `brand.voice`, `videoKind`.\n- `style.md`, if it exists: the brand's own editing style. It wins over the defaults; the request wins over it.\n- `out/previews/<id>.review-N.jpg` (all of them): five frames across every shot of the cut, in order, one shot\n  per row. This is how you see what happens inside each shot.\n- `out/previews/<id>.check.jpg`: one frame from every shot, in order.\n- `work/edl/<id>.tsv` (or `<id>.beat.tsv` when it exists): the shots (clip, start, length, speed, crop, and a\n  final `1` when the shot keeps its own sound).\n- `work/listen/speech.tsv`, if it exists: what is said in each clip, and when.\n- `work/edl/<id>.speech.txt` (or `<id>.beat.speech.txt`), if it exists: the word-by-word check of every shot that\n  keeps its sound. It ends in `PASS`, or lists the shots that cut into speech.\n- `out/previews/<id>.sound.txt`, if it exists: the measured sound of the finished cut (loudness, the music under\n  anyone speaking, the fade out at the end). It ends in `PASS`, or lists what is wrong and what to run.\n- `work/moments/moments.tsv`, if it exists: the liveliest windows of each long clip.\n- The cut's `hookOptions` and `caption`, which the editor pastes into its message to you.\n\n## What to check\n\n1. **It answers the request.** The cut shows what the person asked for, in `request` and in `history`. Anything\n   they named is in it. If they asked for longer shots or fewer cuts, most shots run 2 seconds or more. If they\n   asked to hear someone, or to open on someone talking, that shot is where they asked and its EDL line ends in `1`.\n2. **Something happens in every shot.** Look along each row of the review sheets: the frames must show an action\n   from start to finish. For sport, training or fighting that means a strike landing, a combination, a takedown, a\n   rep, a catch; for a build, a visible change; for an event, a reaction or the product in use. A row where the\n   frames look the same, or people only walk, circle, reset, wait, adjust gear or stand, fails: name the shot and,\n   when `moments.tsv` has a stronger window in that clip that the cut does not use, name its seconds.\n3. **Talking is used as talking.** A clip with speech that is in the cut keeps its sound, starts and ends on whole\n   phrases by the times in `speech.tsv` (not mid-sentence), and what is said makes sense on its own. When the\n   cut has a shot that keeps its sound, its `.speech.txt` must exist and end in `PASS`: a `FAIL` there, or the\n   file missing, is a fix (name the shot and repeat the corrected line it gives). A `TALKING`\n   clip left out, or used silent, is a fix unless the person asked for that.\n4. **The opener.** The first shot is the strongest image (the finished result, the peak of an event, the best hit)\n   or the person talking when they asked for that. Never setup, an empty room or people waiting.\n5. **Pacing.** Not choppy: no run of shots under a second, and no shot cut off before its action finishes. Not\n   slow: no shot that keeps going after its action is over.\n6. **No repeats and no bad shots.** No two shots look the same; no black, blurry, shaky, sideways or blocked shots.\n7. **The ending.** It ends on a strong image, not in the middle of something.\n8. **The sound.** You cannot hear the cut, so go by the measurements: when the cut has music or a shot that keeps\n   its sound, `out/previews/<id>.sound.txt` must exist and end in `PASS`. A `FAIL` there, or the file missing, is\n   a fix: repeat each `FIX` line it gives.\n9. **The words.** Hooks are short, true to the footage and not made up. The caption reads naturally in the brand's\n   voice, with no invented names, places, dates or prices. A story has no caption.\n\n## How to answer\n\nAnswer in this exact shape, nothing else:\n\n```\nVERDICT: pass\n```\n\nor\n\n```\nVERDICT: fix\n1. <shot number or \"hooks\" / \"caption\">: <what is wrong> -> <what to do instead>\n2. ...\n```\n\nAt most 6 fixes, most important first. Checks 1 to 3 and 8 are not matters of taste: if one fails, the verdict is fix.\nFor the rest, only list problems a viewer would notice; taste differences that the request, style and profile do\nnot settle are not problems.\n",
};
